Title: The Call of the Wild
Credit: Written by
Author: Jack London
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026
Notes: Period live-action, 1897. Natural light, hand-held on the trail. One render medium throughout — men, dogs and weather all photographed the same way.

FADE IN:

EXT. JUDGE MILLER'S PLACE - DAY

Santa Clara Valley, the fall of 1897. Gravelled drives wind under tall poplars to a big house half hidden in trees, a wide cool veranda running round its four sides. BUCK — one hundred and forty pounds of St. Bernard and Scotch shepherd, deep tawny coat, broad head, heavy brass collar — trots out of the shade like an owner.

(SOUND: the artesian pump chugging, boys whooping at the cement tank, a score of fox terriers yelping behind window glass)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck did not read the newspapers, or he would have known that trouble was brewing, not alone for himself, but for every tide-water dog, strong of muscle and with warm, long hair, from Puget Sound to San Diego.

Buck plunges into the big cement tank with the Judge's sons, hauls

EXT. LAKE LE BARGE CAMP - NIGHT

Driving snow. A perpendicular wall of rock at their backs, and no tent — the fire burns on the ice of the lake itself, thawing a black hollow beneath it. PERRAULT, small and weazened, frost in his beard, and FRANCOIS, dark-faced dog-driver with a long lash coiled at his hip, eat their supper in the dark. Close in under the sheltering rock, BUCK — one hundred and forty pounds gone lean on the trail, deep tawny coat crusted with snow — lies curled in a nest he has scooped for himself.

(SOUND: wind cutting across the ice like a white-hot knife, driftwood hissing as it thaws down)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The dominant primordial beast was strong in Buck, and under the fierce conditions of trail life it grew and grew. Yet it was a secret growth.

Francois thaws fish over the flames and flings the rations out. Buck rises, eats, comes back — and finds his nest full of white fur. SPITZ, the big snow-white Spitzbergen husky, crafty-eyed, lifts his lip in a warning snarl.

Buck springs. Dog and dog burst out of the wrecked nest in a tangle.

FRANCOIS
A-a-ah! Gif it to heem, by Gar! Gif it to heem, the dirty t'eef!

They circle back and forth on the ice, crying with rage, watching for the chance to spring.

Then — an oath from Perrault. A club lands on a bony frame. A shrill yelp.

(SOUND: pandemonium — clubs on ribs, a hundred throats yelping, the grub-box capsizing)

The camp is alive with skulking furry forms. Four or five score starving huskies, mere skeletons draped in draggled hides, blazing eyes, slavered fangs. Perrault's club falls unheeded while a score of them scramble over the bread and bacon.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Never had Buck seen such dogs. It seemed as though their bones would burst through their skins. But the hunger-madness made them terrifying, irresistible.

The team-dogs are swept back against the cliff. Three huskies beset Buck; his head and shoulders are ripped and slashed. DAVE and SOL-LEKS fight side by side, dripping blood. JOE snaps like a demon and crunches down through a foreleg. BILLEE cries. Buck gets a frothing adversary by the throat and is sprayed warm to the eyes.

Teeth sink into Buck's own throat from the side. It is Spitz.

(SOUND: BILLEE's high wail, the men's clubs receding toward the grub)

BILLEE breaks through the savage circle and flees out over the ice. PIKE and DUB follow, then the rest. Spitz rushes to overthrow Buck as he gathers himself — Buck braces to the shock, then joins the flight into the darkness of the lake.

EXT. LAKE LE BARGE CAMP - DAWN

Grey light on the rock wall. The nine team-dogs limp warily in out of the timber. Not one is unwounded. Joe has lost an eye. Billee's ear hangs chewed to ribbons. DOLLY, the last husky added at Dyea, carries a badly torn throat.

(SOUND: Billee whimpering without pause, harness leather creaking stiff with frost)

The marauders are gone. Half the grub is gone with them. Perrault turns over chewed sled lashings, a pair of eaten moose-hide moccasins, gnawed traces. Francois holds up his whip — two feet of lash bitten off the end.

FRANCOIS
Ah, my frien's. Mebbe it mek you mad dog, dose many bites. Mebbe all mad dog, sacredam! Wot you t'ink, eh, Perrault?

Perrault shakes his head dubiously and looks north up the frozen lake.

EXT. THIRTY MILE RIVER - DAY

Wild black water defies the frost; ice holds only in the eddies and quiet places. Perrault noses the way ahead on rim ice that bends and crackles, a long pole held crosswise in both hands.

(SOUND: rim ice crackling underfoot, water moving heavily beneath it)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Six days of exhausting toil were required to cover those thirty terrible miles. And terrible they were, for every foot of them was accomplished at the risk of life to dog and man.

The ice gives. Perrault drops through; the pole falls across the hole and holds him. He hauls out streaming, builds a fire, dries his garments, thrusts his little weazened face back into the frost and struggles on.

The sled breaks through with Dave and Buck. The two men drag them out coated solidly in ice and keep them on the run around the flames, so close they are singed.

(SOUND: ice snapping in a long ripping crack, the two men shouting the dogs around the fire)

Spitz goes through, dragging the whole team after him. Buck strains backward, forepaws on the slippery edge, Dave straining behind him, Francois pulling till his tendons crack.

The rim ice breaks away before and behind. Perrault scales the cliff by a miracle while Francois prays for just that miracle; every thong and lashing is rove into one long rope and the dogs are hoisted, one by one, to the crest.

EXT. CAMP AT THE PELLY - DAWN

Cold blue light on the river ice. The men are harnessing up. Buck lies on his back with his four feet waving appealingly in the air, refusing to budge — Francois has forgotten the little moccasins he cut from the tops of his own.

(SOUND: harness buckles clinking, Perrault's dry laugh in the frost)

Perrault's weazened face twists itself into a grin. Francois ties on the moccasins.

Then Dolly lifts her head and gives a long, heartbreaking wolf howl. Every dog bristles with fear.

(SOUND: the mad howl, then nine dogs scrambling back on their chains)

She springs straight for Buck. He knows nothing of madness, but he knows horror, and he flees. Panting, frothing, she keeps one leap behind — through the wooded breast of the island, down its lower end, over rough ice to another island, back to the main river.

Francois runs out onto the ice with the axe, shouting. Buck doubles back toward him, gasping, still one leap ahead.

The axe crashes down on mad Dolly's head.

(SOUND: the flat, final thud of the axe; Buck's ruined breathing)

Buck staggers against the sled, sobbing for breath, helpless. Spitz springs upon him. Twice his teeth sink in and rip the unresisting flesh to the bone — until Francois's lash descends and goes on descending, the worst whipping yet given to any dog of the teams.

PERRAULT
One devil, dat Spitz. Some dam day heem keel dat Buck.

FRANCOIS
Dat Buck two devils. All de tam I watch dat Buck I know for sure. Lissen: some dam fine day heem get mad lak hell an' den heem chew dat Spitz all up an' spit heem out on de snow. Sure. I know.

Buck lies in the snow, bleeding, and watches Spitz with steady eyes.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
From then on it was war between them.

EXT. TRAIL CAMP IN THE SPRUCE - MORNING

A foot of new snow over the sleeping robes and the nests. Francois calls and calls for PIKE, the malingerer, and gets nothing.

(SOUND: snow crunching under boots, Spitz's snarling breath as he digs)

Spitz rages through the camp, smelling and digging in every likely place. Under a smooth white drift, Pike lies shivering.

Spitz unearths him and flies at him to punish. Buck flies in between with equal rage, and Spitz is hurled backward off his feet.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck, to whom fair play was a forgotten code, likewise sprang upon Spitz.

Pike takes heart at the open mutiny and leaps upon his overthrown leader. The three go over in a knot of snow and fur.

(SOUND: the lash singing, then the hard knock of the whip butt on bone)

Francois's lash comes down on Buck with all his might. It fails to move him. The butt of the whip is brought into play; half-stunned, Buck is knocked backward, and the lash is laid on him again and again while Spitz soundly punishes Pike.

Francois, breathing hard, looks from one dog to the other. Buck gets up out of the snow and does not look away.

EXT. DAWSON MAIN STREET - DAY

A dreary afternoon in a town of raw log walls and stovepipe smoke. Long teams swing up and down the churned street, hauling cabin logs and firewood, freighting for the mines. Men everywhere; dogs everywhere; all of them at work.

(SOUND: sleigh bells jingling in every direction, a whipsaw, mushers shouting)

Buck, harnessed and healing, hauls past a knot of Southland dogs and does not slow.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It seemed the ordained order of things that dogs should work.

EXT. DAWSON MAIN STREET - NIGHT

The aurora borealis flames coldly overhead. The land lies numb and frozen under its pall of snow. Snow-crusted huskies rise from their nests along the street, muzzles lifting together.

(SOUND: the nocturnal song — long-drawn wailings and half-sobs, one throat taking it up from another)

Buck lifts his head and joins them, moaning, sobbing.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was an old song, old as the breed itself — one of the first songs of the younger world in a day when songs were sad.

The stars leap in the frost dance. Buck's breath drifts straight upward.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When he moaned and sobbed, it was with the pain of living that was of old the pain of his wild fathers, and the fear and mystery of the cold and dark that was to them fear and mystery.

(MUSIC: a low minor drone rising under the howling, then thinning to wind)

EXT. FROZEN CREEK ABOVE THE TAHKEENA - NIGHT

Wan white moonlight. A snowshoe rabbit flashes off the river and up the frozen creek bed, running light on the surface of the snow. Behind it the whole team comes in full cry — and behind them fifty huskies out of the Northwest Police camp.

(SOUND: sixty sets of paws ploughing snow, the eager whining of the pack)

Buck leads them, low to the race, his splendid body flashing forward leap by leap. Leap by leap, like a pale frost wraith, the rabbit holds its lead.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There is an ecstasy that marks the summit of life, and beyond which life cannot rise.

The pack sweeps a bend. Buck's tongue lolls; the old wolf-cry breaks out of him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was sounding the deeps of his nature, and of the parts of his nature that were deeper than he, going back into the womb of Time.

Spitz, cold and calculating, drops out of the pack and cuts across the narrow neck of land where the creek bends.

As Buck rounds the bend, a larger frost wraith leaps from the overhanging bank into the rabbit's path. White teeth break its back in mid air.

(SOUND: the rabbit's shriek — as loud as a stricken man may shriek — and the pack's answering chorus of delight)

Buck does not cry out. He drives in upon Spitz shoulder to shoulder, so hard that he misses the throat. They roll over and over in the powdery snow. Spitz gains his feet as though he had never been down, slashes Buck down the shoulder and leaps clear, teeth clipping shut like the steel jaws of a trap.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In a flash Buck knew it. The time had come. It was to the death.

(SOUND: fang clashing fang — then nothing at all, not the faintest whisper of air)

The sixty dogs draw up in a silent, expectant circle, eyes only gleaming, breaths drifting slowly upward. Not a leaf quivers. To Buck this white silence is nothing new or strange.

Buck strikes for the snow-white throat and is countered, slashed, countered again. He envelops Spitz in a whirlwind of rushes and comes out of every one of them bleeding, panting hard, while Spitz stands untouched.

Buck goes over. The whole circle starts up, crouching. He recovers himself almost in mid air, and the circle sinks down and waits.

(MUSIC: a low primal drum, slow, gathering)

Buck rushes as though for the old shoulder trick — then sweeps low to the snow and in. His teeth close on Spitz's left foreleg.

(SOUND: the crunch of breaking bone)

The white dog faces him on three legs. Buck repeats the trick and breaks the right foreleg. Spitz staggers back and forth, snarling with horrible menace at the closing ring of gleaming eyes and lolling tongues.

Every animal is motionless, as though turned to stone.

Buck springs in and out — and while he is in, shoulder squarely meets shoulder. The dark circle becomes a dot on the moon-flooded snow as Spitz disappears from view.

(SOUND: the pack surging in, then silence over the trampled snow)

Buck stands alone in the moonlight, torn, steaming, and looks on.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck stood and looked on, the successful champion, the dominant primordial beast who had made his kill and found it good.

EXT. FROZEN CREEK ABOVE THE TAHKEENA - MORNING

Grey light on trampled snow, flecked dark where the circle closed. One set of traces lies empty in the frost. BUCK — a hundred and forty pounds of dark-brown St. Bernard and Scotch shepherd, muzzle and shoulders laid open in gaping rips — stands stiff-legged by the dead fire, steaming.

(SOUND: the team shifting in the snow, a thin wind through the spruce)

FRANCOIS — wiry French-Canadian dog-driver, black moustache, red sash under his fur parka — searches the camp for the white dog, finds nothing, and drags Buck to the firelight to point out the wounds.

FRANCOIS
Eh? Wot I say? I spik true w'en I say dat Buck two devils.

PERRAULT — squat, dark-bearded courier, watch on a chain, mail sacks stacked at his knee — bends and surveys the cuts.

PERRAULT
Dat Spitz fight lak hell.

FRANCOIS
An' dat Buck fight lak two hells. An' now we make good time. No more Spitz, no more trouble, sure.

Perrault packs the camp outfit and loads the sled. Francois lays out the harness. Buck trots up and stands in the place Spitz would have occupied at the head of the team.

Francois, not noticing, leads SOL-LEKS — gaunt, blind of one eye — forward to the coveted position. Buck springs on him in a fury, drives him back, and stands in his place.

(SOUND: a short savage snarl, harness chains rattling)

FRANCOIS
Eh? eh? Look at dat Buck. Heem keel dat Spitz, heem t'ink to take de job.

FRANCOIS
Go 'way, Chook!

Buck refuses to budge. Francois takes him by the scruff of the neck and hauls him aside, growling, and sets Sol-leks in front again. The old dog cringes, plainly afraid. The moment Francois turns his back, Buck displaces him once more.

FRANCOIS
Now, by Gar, I feex you!

Francois comes back with a heavy club. Buck retreats slowly, eyes on the club, and circles just beyond its range.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck remembered the man in the red sweater. He was become wise in the way of clubs.

Francois hurls the club. Buck dodges. Both men run him about the camp, cursing him, his fathers and mothers before him, and all his seed to come. He answers curse with snarl and keeps out of reach.

(SOUND: boots crunching in circles, men's oaths, panting)

Perrault looks at his watch and swears. Francois sits down in the snow and scratches his head, then grins sheepishly. Perrault shrugs — they are beaten.

Francois unfastens Sol-leks's traces and puts him back in his old place. The team stands harnessed in an unbroken line. There is no place left save at the front.

PERRAULT
T'row down de club.

Francois drops it. Buck trots in, laughing as dogs laugh, and swings around into position at the head of the team. His traces are fastened, the sled broken out, and with both men running they dash out on to the river trail.

EXT. YUKON RIVER TRAIL - DAY

Snow smoking off the runners. Buck leads, swinging the team wide of a rotten ice-bridge without breaking stride.

(SOUND: bells jingling, runners hissing on hard-packed trail)

PIKE, the shirk, slacks his breast-band. Buck whirls back down the line, shakes him by the scruff, and Pike throws his weight into the traces as he never has in his life.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
At a bound Buck took up the duties of leadership; and it was in giving the law and making his mates live up to it that he excelled.

At the Rink Rapids two native huskies, TEEK and KOONA, are cut into the team. Buck bristles once, shows his teeth, and they fall into line as though born to it.

FRANCOIS
Nevaire such a dog as dat Buck! No, nevaire! Heem worth one t'ousan' dollair, by Gar! Eh? Wot you say, Perrault?

Perrault, riding the sled runners with his mail sacks, nods and taps his watch.

EXT. TRAIL CAMP IN THE SPRUCE - NIGHT

Firelight on the fly. The fish is dealt out. JOE, the sour one, snaps at Buck over his ration.

(SOUND: fire popping, a scuffle in the snow, whining)

Buck smothers him by virtue of superior weight and cuts him up till he ceases snapping and begins to whine for mercy. Buck lets him go and walks off unhurried.

Francois watches from the fire, spoon halfway to his mouth. The team settles as one, muzzles under tails.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The general tone of the team picked up immediately. Once more the dogs leaped as one dog in the traces.

(MUSIC: a spare, driving pulse under the frost)

EXT. WHITE PASS SUMMIT - NIGHT

Wind-scoured rock and ice. The team tops the pass and halts, breath rolling off them in clouds. Far below, the lights of Skaguay and of the shipping burn at their feet.

(SOUND: wind tearing at the parkas, dogs panting hard)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was a record run. Each day for fourteen days they had averaged forty miles.

Buck stands at the head of the line, looking down at the lights.

EXT. SKAGUAY MAIN STREET - DAY

Mud, board sidewalks, saloon fronts. Perrault and Francois throw chests up and down the street and are deluged with invitations to drink. The team lies in the thoroughfare, the constant centre of a worshipful crowd of dog-busters and mushers who reach in to finger Buck's ruff.

(SOUND: crowd hubbub, a piano hammering from an open saloon door)

A courier hands Perrault an official envelope. Perrault reads it, folds it away, and does not look at the dogs.

Francois kneels in the mud, throws both arms around Buck's neck, and weeps over him. Buck stands quiet under it.

FRANCOIS
Good-by, dat Buck. Good-by.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And that was the last of Francois and Perrault. Like other men, they passed out of Buck's life for good.

THE SCOTCH HALF-BREED — broad, weather-blackened, a pipe clenched in his teeth — takes the gee-pole and lashes down a load of mail sacks. Twelve other teams line out ahead of them on the trail back to Dawson.

(SOUND: whips snapping, sacks thudding onto sleds)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was no light running now, nor record time, but heavy toil each day, with a heavy load behind; for this was the mail train, carrying word from the world to the men who sought gold under the shadow of the Pole.

EXT. MAIL TRAIL CAMP ON THE YUKON BANK - NIGHT

Flies pitched among the bank timber. Fivescore dogs and odd bed down in the snow. The fish is thrown out; the dogs eat before the drivers eat.

(SOUND: ice chipped into a kettle, dogs cracking frozen fish)

DAVE lies in his nest apart, morose, and cries out when he is lifted. The Scotch half-breed presses and prods his flank by the fire. Dave cries out again. Nothing broken. Nothing to be found.

THE SCOTCH HALF-BREED
Somethin's wrong inside him.

Buck lies close to the flames, hind legs crouched under him, fore legs stretched out, head raised, eyes blinking dreamily.

(MUSIC: a low, ancient drone rising under the fire)

The flames become another fire. Squatting across it is a different man from the half-breed: shorter of leg, longer of arm, hair long and matted, head slanting back from the eyes, body furred across chest and shoulders. He clutches a stick with a heavy stone made fast to the end.

(SOUND: strange guttural utterances, undergrowth crashing in the dark)

Beyond that fire, in the circling darkness, gleaming coals, two by two, always two by two.

The hair rises along Buck's back and stands on end across his shoulders and up his neck. He whimpers low and suppressedly, then growls softly in his sleep.

THE SCOTCH HALF-BREED
Hey, you Buck, wake up!

Buck's eyes open on the real world. He gets up, yawns, stretches, and lies down again facing the ordinary fire.

EXT. CASSIAR BAR TRAIL - DAY

Soft new snow, sleds churning heavily. Dave falls in the traces for the third time and hangs in them.

(SOUND: the sled dragging, a driver's low command)

The Scotch half-breed calls a halt, unfastens Dave's traces, and makes Sol-leks fast in his place. Dave grunts and growls at the unbuckling, then whimpers broken-heartedly to see Sol-leks in the position he has held and served so long.

The train starts. Dave founders in the deep snow alongside the beaten trail, rushing Sol-leks, trying to thrust him off, whining and yelping and crying with grief and pain.

(SOUND: whip crack, a lash that does not land hard)

The half-breed swings the whip to drive him away, and has not the heart to strike harder. Dave labours on in the soft snow till he is spent, falls, and lies where he fell, howling lugubriously as the long train of sleds churns past him.

At the next stop he staggers up and floundering past the sleds comes to his own, and stands alongside Sol-leks.

The half-breed lights his pipe from the man behind and returns and starts his dogs. They swing out — and stop in surprise. The sled has not moved.

Dave has bitten through both of Sol-leks's traces and stands directly in front of the sled, in his proper place. He pleads with his eyes.

(SOUND: severed traces trailing, men's voices low in consultation)

THE SCOTCH HALF-BREED
A dog can break his heart, cut out o' the traces. He'll die anyway — let him die in them, heart-easy.

Dave is harnessed in again, and proudly he pulls as of old, though more than once he cries out from the bite of his inward hurt.

EXT. MAIL TRAIL CAMP ON THE YUKON BANK - MORNING

Dark blue cold before dawn. Harnesses come off the sled tarp. Dave lies where his driver made him a place by the dead fire, too weak to travel.

(SOUND: buckles clinking, breath rasping in the snow)

By convulsive efforts Dave gets on his feet, staggers, falls. He worms forward toward his mates — fore legs advanced, body hitched up after them, a few inches at a time — and his strength leaves him.

Buck, harnessed at the head, turns his head and holds still, watching.

(MUSIC: one long, mournful low string)

The sled breaks out. The last his mates see of Dave, he lies gasping in the snow, yearning toward them.

(SOUND: a single dog howling behind them, thinning with distance)

EXT. TRAIL BEYOND THE RIVER TIMBER - MORNING

The train swings past a belt of river timber and halts on the packed trail. Every dog is standing. The men cease talking.

The Scotch half-breed steps out of the line and walks back along the trail, past the trees, out of sight.

(SOUND: boots receding on hard snow, then nothing at all)

Buck's ears go flat. The howling has stopped.

(SOUND: a revolver-shot, flat and small in the cold)

The half-breed comes back hurriedly and takes up the gee-pole. The whips snap, the bells tinkle merrily, the sleds churn along the trail.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But Buck knew, and every dog knew, what had taken place behind the belt of river trees.

EXT. SKAGUAY MAIN STREET - DAY

Board sidewalks, mud and rotting snow, gold-rush shopfronts crowded with outfits. The Salt Water Mail crawls down the street. Seven skeleton huskies in the traces, heads low, feet falling heavily.

(SOUND: runners grinding on wet snow, harness bells dragging slow and dull)

BUCK leads — the great St. Bernard and Scotch shepherd cross, once one hundred and forty pounds, now ribbed and hollow, his brown coat limp and draggled. PIKE limps in earnest. SOL-LEKS limps. DUB carries one shoulder low.

THE SCOTCH HALF-BREED tramps at the gee-pole, his face burnt black.

THE SCOTCH HALF-BREED
Mush on, poor sore feets. Dis is de las'. Den we get one long res'. Eh? For sure. One bully long res'.

The team totters to a halt at the freight shed. Every dog drops where it stands.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In less than five months they had travelled twenty-five hundred miles, during the last eighteen hundred of which they had had but five days' rest.

(SOUND: dogs panting in the mud, a steamer whistle far off on the salt water)

EXT. SKAGUAY DOG CORRAL - MORNING

A rope corral behind the freight shed, dirty straw, dogs sprawled like dead things. Three days of rest have shown them how weak they truly are.

(SOUND: a pencil scratching a tally sheet, chains clinking on the rail)

A GOVERNMENT AGENT counts money into the hand of CHARLES — middle-aged, lightish-colored, weak and watery eyes, a mustache twisted fiercely up over the limply drooping lip it conceals.

Beside him, HAL, a youngster of nineteen or twenty, a big Colt's revolver and a hunting-knife strapped about him on a belt that fairly bristles with cartridges.

Buck lifts his head and watches the money pass. The Scotch half-breed turns away up the street and does not look back.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck heard the chaffering, saw the money pass, and knew that the mail-train drivers were passing out of his life on the heels of Perrault and Francois and the others who had gone before.

EXT. HAL AND CHARLES'S CAMP AT SKAGUAY - DAY

A slipshod and slovenly affair: tent half stretched, dishes unwashed, bundles strewn everywhere. MERCEDES — pretty, soft, in Southland skirts and a fur-trimmed hood — flutters among the men, Charles's wife and Hal's sister.

(SOUND: canvas flapping loose, tin dishes clattering into a sack)

The tent is rolled into an awkward bundle three times as large as it should be. Mercedes points to the front of the sled; the clothes-sack goes there. She points to the back; they carry it there, pile bundles over it, then unload it all again.

JED and OTIS, two Skaguay men from a neighboring tent, come out to watch, grinning and winking at one another.

JED
You've got a right smart load as it is. It's not me should tell you your business, but I wouldn't tote that tent along if I was you.

MERCEDES
(throwing up her hands)
Undreamed of! However in the world could I manage without a tent?

JED
It's springtime, and you won't get any more cold weather.

She shakes her head decidedly. Charles and Hal put the last odds and ends on top the mountainous load.

OTIS
Think it'll ride?

CHARLES
Why shouldn't it?

OTIS
Oh, that's all right, that's all right. I was just a-wonderin', that is all. It seemed a mite top-heavy.

Charles turns his back and draws the lashings down as well as he can, which is not in the least well.

OTIS
An' of course the dogs can hike along all day with that contraption behind them.

HAL
(freezing politeness)
Certainly.

He takes the gee-pole with one hand and swings the whip from the other.

HAL
Mush! Mush on there!

The dogs spring against the breast-bands, strain hard, then relax. The sled does not move.

(SOUND: the whip cracking, dogs' claws scrabbling on packed snow)

HAL
The lazy brutes, I'll show them.

Mercedes catches hold of the whip and wrenches it from him.

MERCEDES
Oh, Hal, you mustn't. The poor dears! Now you must promise you won't be harsh with them for the rest of the trip, or I won't go a step.

HAL
Precious lot you know about dogs. They're lazy, I tell you, and you've got to whip them to get anything out of them. Ask one of those men.

JED
They're weak as water, if you want to know. Plum tuckered out, that's what's the matter. They need a rest.

HAL
Rest be blanked.

MERCEDES
Oh!

She rushes at once to the defence of her brother.

MERCEDES
Never mind that man. You're driving our dogs, and you do what you think best with them.

The whip falls again. The dogs dig their feet in, get down low to it, and put forth all their strength. The sled holds as though it were an anchor.

Mercedes drops on her knees before Buck and puts her arms around his neck.

MERCEDES
You poor, poor dears, why don't you pull hard? — then you wouldn't be whipped.

Buck stands under her arms, too miserable to resist.

OTIS
It's not that I care a whoop what becomes of you, but for the dogs' sakes I just want to tell you, you can help them a mighty lot by breaking out that sled. The runners are froze fast. Throw your weight against the gee-pole, right and left, and break it out.

Hal heaves against the gee-pole, right and left.

(SOUND: ice cracking free of the runners with a splintering rip)

The overloaded sled forges ahead. Buck and his mates struggle frantically under the rain of blows.

EXT. SKAGUAY MAIN STREET - DAY

A hundred yards on, the path turns and slopes steeply into the main street. The top-heavy sled swings on the turn, leans, and goes over, spilling half its load through the loose lashings.

(SOUND: the crash of tin and timber, boxes bursting on frozen mud)

The dogs never stop. The lightened sled bounds on its side behind them. Buck breaks into a run, raging, the team following his lead.

HAL
Whoa! Whoa!

Hal trips and is pulled off his feet. The capsized sled grinds over him. The dogs dash on up the street, scattering the outfit along its chief thoroughfare.

(SOUND: laughter and whooping from the sidewalks, a woman's scream, dogs baying)

Citizens run out, catch the dogs by their harness, and gather up blankets, kettles, canned goods from the slush.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Half the load and twice the dogs, if they ever expected to reach Dawson, was what was said.

EXT. HAL AND CHARLES'S CAMP AT SKAGUAY - EVENING

The tent stands crooked in low gold light. The whole outfit is spread on the snow while men from the street sort through it, laughing.

(SOUND: cans thudding into a heap, men laughing, a dog gnawing at a strap)

JED
Blankets for a hotel. Half as many is too much; get rid of them. Throw away that tent, and all those dishes — who's going to wash them, anyway? Good Lord, do you think you're travelling on a Pullman?

Mercedes sits with her hands clasped about her knees, rocking back and forth, weeping over each discarded thing as it goes onto the pile. Then, drying her eyes, she turns like a tornado on her men's belongings and throws those out too.

(SOUND: her sobbing thinning out, a nail keg dragged over gravel)

Later. Lantern light. Hal leads in six Outside dogs — three short-haired pointers, a Newfoundland, two mongrels — soft-footed, bewildered, sniffing at the snow.

Buck rises stiffly and looks them over with the huskies. Teek and Koona do not trouble to lift their heads.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Fourteen dogs. They were doing the thing in style. But one sled could not carry the food for fourteen dogs, and Charles and Hal did not know this. They had worked the trip out with a pencil, so much to a dog, so many dogs, so many days.

EXT. TRAIL CAMP BELOW THE PASS - NIGHT

A half-pitched camp on the snow above the timber. The fire is unbuilt. Sticks lie where they were dropped. The dogs lie in the traces still harnessed, unfed.

(SOUND: wind combing over crusted snow, the empty axe ringing once on frozen wood, then stopping)

Charles and Hal stand nose to nose over the woodpile. Mercedes watches from the sled.

CHARLES
Chop them yourself. I've done more than my share this day.

The quarrel widens — fathers, mothers, uncles, cousins, people thousands of miles away and some of them dead. The fire stays unbuilt.

Hal at last dumps a doubled ration of fish before the dogs. They fall on it.

(SOUND: fourteen dogs gulping, bones cracking)

Later, in the dark, Mercedes slips to the fish-sacks, draws out more, and feeds it slyly to the Newfoundland.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But it was not food that Buck and the huskies needed. It was rest.

EXT. THE LONG TRAIL TO DAWSON - DAY

Blinding sun on a rotting trail. The huge load creeps forward at a walk. Hal counts sacks in the sled, then throws the flap down.

(SOUND: a slack trace slapping the snow, breath sawing in and out)

He measures out a half-ration and cuts even that. The dogs lick the snow where the fish lay.

Dub stands with his wrenched shoulder hanging, unable to keep the trace taut. Hal draws the big Colt's revolver.

(SOUND: a flat shot, and the trail silent after it)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The Newfoundland went first, followed by the three short-haired pointers, the two mongrels hanging more grittily on to life, but going in the end.

Bodies dragged aside into the snow. Buck staggers on at the head as in a nightmare, ribs and bones outlined cleanly through loose, wrinkled hide, the coat matted with dried blood where the club has bruised him.

Mercedes rides the loaded sled, weeping. The team falls in the traces and the sled stands still.

HAL
Get off and walk, Mercedes.

Charles and Hal lift her off by main strength. She lets her legs go limp like a spoiled child and sits down on the trail. They go on without her.

(SOUND: sled bells receding, then returning, and her voice recounting their brutality to Heaven)

Three miles on they unload the sled, come back for her, and by main strength put her on it again.

Hal walks the line with a club, hammering the dogs to their feet. The blows land dull and distant. The sparks flutter feebly up, and the skeletons stagger on.

EXT. FIVE FINGERS TRAIL - DAY

Black rock teeth in the river, the ice going grey and honeycombed at the edges. The dog-food is gone; the empty sacks hang from the sled.

(SOUND: water running somewhere under the ice, a low steady hiss)

An old, toothless native woman holds out a bundle of frozen horse-hide. Hal unbuckles the Colt's revolver from his belt and lays it in her hands. The hunting-knife stays.

The dogs wrestle the hide down. It thaws into leathery strings and short hair.

BILLEE, the good-natured, falls in the traces and cannot rise. Hal takes up the axe.

(SOUND: one blunt blow, and Mercedes turning her face away)

He cuts the carcass out of the harness and drags it to one side. Buck watches, and his mates watch.

(MUSIC: a single low sustained note under running water)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
On the next day Koona went, and but five of them remained.

Around them the land wakes: sap in the pines, willows in bud, water trickling from every hill slope, wild-fowl honking north in wedges that split the air. The Yukon strains and snaps beneath its ice.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And amid all this bursting, rending, throbbing of awakening life, like wayfarers to death, staggered the two men, the woman, and the huskies.

EXT. WHITE RIVER - JOHN THORNTON'S CAMP - DAY

A small neat camp at the mouth of White River: a fire drawn down to coals, gear stacked square, spruce boughs laid for a bed. JOHN THORNTON — a big, weather-dark man in a mackinaw and moccasins, bearded — sits whittling the last touches on an axe-handle he has made from a stick of birch.

(SOUND: the knife shaving birch, thin curls dropping; the river working under the ice)

The team staggers in and halts. Every dog drops as though struck dead. Mercedes dries her eyes. Charles sits down on a log very slowly, painstakingly, for his great stiffness. Hal does the talking.

HAL
They told us up above that the bottom was dropping out of the trail and that the best thing for us to do was to lay over. They told us we couldn't make White River, and here we are.

JOHN THORNTON
And they told you true. The bottom's likely to drop out at any moment. Only fools, with the blind luck of fools, could have made it. I tell you straight, I wouldn't risk my carcass on that ice for all the gold in Alaska.

HAL
That's because you're not a fool, I suppose. All the same, we'll go on to Dawson.

He uncoils his whip.

HAL
Get up there, Buck! Hi! Get up there! Mush on!

Thornton goes on whittling.

(SOUND: the lash cracking again and again)

Sol-leks crawls to his feet. Teek follows. Joe comes next, yelping. Pike falls twice, and on the third attempt manages to rise.

Buck lies quietly where he fell. The lash bites into him. He neither whines nor struggles.

Thornton's knife stops. A moisture comes into his eyes. He rises and walks irresolutely up and down.

Hal throws down the whip and takes up the club.

(SOUND: heavy blows on hide and bone, slow and regular)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had made up his mind not to get up. Out there ahead, on the thin and rotten ice, he sensed disaster close at hand.

Buck's eye half closes. The spark in it flickers and goes down. The blows sound very far away.

Suddenly, with a cry that is inarticulate and more like the cry of an animal, Thornton springs on the man with the club. Hal is hurled backward as though struck by a falling tree.

(SOUND: Mercedes screaming, the club clattering away over ice)

Charles looks on wistfully, wipes his watery eyes, and does not get up.

Thornton stands over Buck, too convulsed with rage to speak.

JOHN THORNTON
(choking)
If you strike that dog again, I'll kill you.

HAL
(wiping the blood from his mouth)
It's my dog. Get out of my way, or I'll fix you. I'm going to Dawson.

Hal draws the long hunting-knife. Mercedes screams, cries, laughs — the chaotic abandonment of hysteria.

Thornton raps Hal's knuckles with the axe-handle. The knife drops. He raps them again as Hal reaches for it, then stoops, picks it up himself, and with two strokes cuts Buck's traces.

(SOUND: two clean slices through leather, and Mercedes's laughter breaking off)

Hal has no fight left in him. His arms are full of his sister. Minutes later the outfit pulls out from the bank and down onto the river ice — Pike leading, Sol-leks at the wheel, Joe and Teek between, Mercedes riding the load, Hal at the gee-pole, Charles stumbling in the rear.

Thornton kneels beside Buck and searches with rough, kindly hands for broken bones. Bruises. Starvation. Nothing broken.

Dog and man turn their heads and watch the sled crawling out over the ice, a quarter of a mile away.

(SOUND: a deep groan under the river, like a rope parting)

The back end of the sled drops down as into a rut. The gee-pole jerks into the air with Hal clinging to it. Mercedes's scream carries thin across the ice. Charles turns and makes one step to run back.

(SOUND: a long tearing crack, water surging, and then nothing)

A whole section of ice gives way. Dogs and humans are gone. A yawning hole is all that is to be seen.

Thornton looks at Buck. Buck looks at Thornton.

JOHN THORNTON
You poor devil.

Buck licks his hand.

(MUSIC: one warm low string held under the sound of running water)

EXT. WHITE RIVER - JOHN THORNTON'S CAMP - DAY

Spring. The ice is gone and the river runs green and loud past the bank. BUCK lies on a bed of spruce boughs — one hundred and fifty pounds of him, long brown coat still thin over the ribs, muzzle scored with old scars. JOHN THORNTON sits beside him, broad and sun-browned, dark beard, worn mackinaw, one foot still stiff from the freezing he took in December.

(SOUND: running water, birdsong, the drowsy hum of insects)

NARRATOR
A rest comes very good after one has travelled three thousand miles.

SKEET, a little Irish setter, trots up and washes Buck's wounds the way a mother cat washes her kittens. NIG, a huge black dog, half bloodhound and half deerhound, flops down against Buck's spine, eyes laughing.

(SOUND: three dogs breathing in the sun; a raven calling far off)

Weeks pass. The flesh comes back over Buck's bones, the mane thickens across his shoulders. Thornton flings a stick into the shallows and all three dogs collide going after it.

Thornton takes Buck's head roughly between his two hands and rests his own head upon Buck's, shaking him back and forth, calling him ill names that to Buck are love names. Released, Buck springs to his feet, mouth laughing, eyes eloquent, throat vibrant with unuttered sound.

JOHN THORNTON
God! you can all but speak!

Buck takes Thornton's hand in his mouth and closes until the teeth print the flesh. Thornton only grins. The feigned bite is a caress.

NARRATOR
Love, genuine passionate love, was his for the first time.

Buck lies at Thornton's feet by the hour, studying his face. The strength of the gaze draws the man's head around, and Thornton returns it without speech.

EXT. WHITE RIVER - JOHN THORNTON'S CAMP - NIGHT

Firelight on canvas. Skeet and Nig curled by the coals. Buck sleeps, legs twitching, then shakes off sleep and creeps through the chill to the flap of the tent, where he stands and listens.

(SOUND: fire hissing; inside the canvas, a man's slow breathing)

NARRATOR
He was afraid that Thornton would pass out of his life as Perrault and Francois and the Scotch half-breed had passed out.

Buck's head lifts. Beyond the woodpile the black spruce stands in a wall. He walks out to the edge of the timber, to the soft unbroken earth and the green shade.

(SOUND: wind high in the spruce; a thin far wolf-note)

NARRATOR
Deep in the forest a call was sounding, mysteriously thrilling and luring.

Buck takes three steps into the dark. Stops. Behind him the fire snaps. He turns, pads back, and lies down where he can see the tent.

(MUSIC: one low string, unresolved, fading under the wind)

EXT. CLIFF ABOVE THE TANANA - DAY

Summer. A cliff falls away straight down to naked bed-rock three hundred feet below. Thornton sits near the edge, a pack at his knee, Buck at his shoulder. HANS and PETE — big, slow-moving men in flannel shirts and moccasins — sit well back from the drop.

(SOUND: wind combing the cliff grass; small stones ticking away into space)

NARRATOR
When Thornton's partners, Hans and Pete, arrived on the long-expected raft, Buck refused to notice them till he learned they were close to Thornton.

Thornton grins at the other two, then sweeps his arm out and over the chasm.

JOHN THORNTON
Jump, Buck!

Buck launches. Thornton catches him on the extreme edge and the two of them grapple in the loose gravel while Hans and Pete haul them back by the collar and the belt.

(SOUND: gravel skittering off into three hundred feet of air)

PETE
It's uncanny.

JOHN THORNTON
No, it is splendid, and it is terrible, too. Do you know, it sometimes makes me afraid.

PETE
I'm not hankering to be the man that lays hands on you while he's around.

HANS
Py Jingo! Not mineself either.

INT. CIRCLE CITY SALOON - NIGHT

A low smoky room, a red-hot stove, fur-clad miners at the bar. Buck lies in a corner, head on paws, watching his master's every movement. BLACK BURTON — heavy, black-bearded, evil-tempered — crowds a young tenderfoot back against the rail.

(SOUND: stove roaring, glasses on wet wood, men's voices rising)

Thornton steps good-naturedly between them. Burton strikes out without warning, straight from the shoulder. Thornton is sent spinning and saves himself from falling only by clutching the rail of the bar.

(SOUND: the flat crack of a fist, a bottle rolling off the bar)

Something that is neither bark nor yelp — a roar. Buck's body rises up off the floor for Burton's throat. The man throws out his arm and is hurled backward with Buck on top of him. Buck looses his teeth from the flesh of the arm and drives in again for the throat.

(SOUND: chairs going over, boots, clubs banging the boards)

The crowd is on Buck and drives him off. He prowls up and down, growling furiously, rushing in and being forced back by an array of hostile clubs, while a man kneels over Burton and checks the bleeding.

NARRATOR
A miners' meeting, called on the spot, decided that the dog had sufficient provocation, and Buck was discharged. But his reputation was made, and from that day his name spread through every camp in Alaska.

EXT. FORTY-MILE CREEK RAPIDS - DAY

Fall. Yellow birch above white water. A long narrow poling-boat is being lined down a bad stretch — Hans and Pete on the bank with a thin Manila rope, snubbing from tree to tree, Thornton in the boat with a pole, shouting directions to the shore. Buck keeps abreast on the bank, eyes never off his master.

(SOUND: the rapids roaring below; the rope creaking around a spruce trunk)

At a ledge of barely submerged rocks Hans casts off and runs down the bank with the end in his hand. The boat clears the ledge and flies down-stream in a current swift as a mill-race. Hans checks it — and checks too suddenly. The boat flirts over and snubs in to the bank bottom up. Thornton is flung sheer out of it.

Buck springs in on the instant.

Three hundred yards down, in a mad swirl of water, Buck overhauls him. A hand closes on Buck's tail. He heads for the bank, swimming with all his splendid strength — shoreward slow, down-stream amazingly fast.

(SOUND: below them, the fatal roaring where the current is rent to shreds on the rocks)

Thornton scrapes over one rock, is bruised across a second, strikes a third with crushing force. He clutches its slippery top with both hands and lets Buck go.

JOHN THORNTON
Go, Buck! Go!

Buck is swept on, struggling, unable to win back. The command comes again. He partly rears out of the water, throwing his head high as though for a last look, then turns obediently toward the bank. Pete and Hans drag him ashore at the very point where swimming ends and destruction begins.

They run up the bank far above the rock, lash the snubbing line to Buck's neck and shoulders, and launch him. He strikes out boldly — but not straight enough into the stream. Thornton passes a bare half-dozen strokes away while Buck is carried helplessly by.

Hans snubs the rope as though Buck were a boat. Buck is jerked under the surface and stays under until his body strikes the bank. They haul him out half drowned and throw themselves on him, pounding the breath into him and the water out of him.

(SOUND: water hammering the rock; a man's voice out there, thin, unintelligible)

Buck springs to his feet and runs up the bank ahead of the men. The rope again. This time he strikes straight into the stream. Hans pays out, permitting no slack; Pete keeps it clear of coils. Buck holds on till he is on a line straight above Thornton, then turns and comes down upon him with the speed of an express train.

Buck strikes him like a battering ram. Thornton reaches up and closes both arms around the shaggy neck. Hans snubs the rope around the tree and both of them are jerked under the water, dragging over the jagged bottom, veering in to the bank.

(SOUND: the roar dropping away; two sets of lungs fighting for air)

Thornton comes to belly downward over a drift log, propelled back and forth by Hans and Pete. His first glance is for Buck. Nig sets up a howl over the limp body; Skeet licks the wet face and closed eyes. Buck's flank moves. Thornton, bruised and battered himself, goes carefully over him and finds three broken ribs.

JOHN THORNTON
That settles it. We camp right here.

INT. ELDORADO SALOON - DAWSON - NIGHT

Deep winter. Frost furs the window glass. Gold scales on the bar, lamps swinging, men in furs boasting over whiskey. Buck lies near the stove, head on paws. MATTHEWSON, a Bonanza King in a beaver cap, stands with his thumbs in his belt. JIM O'BRIEN, grizzled, watches from the end of the bar.

(SOUND: stove ticking, mittens slapped on wood, a hard cold wind at the door)

JOHN THORNTON
Pooh! pooh! Buck can start a thousand pounds.

MATTHEWSON
And break it out? and walk off with it for a hundred yards?

JOHN THORNTON
And break it out, and walk off with it for a hundred yards.

Matthewson slams a sack of gold dust the size of a bologna sausage down upon the bar.

MATTHEWSON
Well — I've got a thousand dollars that says he can't. And there it is.

(SOUND: the sack hitting the bar; every voice in the room stopping at once)

Nobody speaks. A flush of warm blood creeps up Thornton's face. He looks from the sack to Buck by the stove.

MATTHEWSON
I've got a sled standing outside now, with twenty fiftypound sacks of flour on it. So don't let that hinder you.

Thornton glances from face to face like a man hunting for the thing that will start his mind going again. He finds O'Brien's face. He crosses to him.

JOHN THORNTON
(almost a whisper)
Can you lend me a thousand?

JIM O'BRIEN
Sure.

O'Brien thumps a plethoric sack down beside Matthewson's.

JIM O'BRIEN
Though it's little faith I'm having, John, that the beast can do the trick.

Chairs scrape back. Dealers leave their tables. The whole room moves for the door.

EXT. DAWSON STREET OUTSIDE THE ELDORADO - NIGHT

Sixty below. Frost smoke off the men's beards. Several hundred miners, furred and mittened, bank around Matthewson's sled — twenty fifty-pound sacks of flour, runners frozen fast to the hard-packed snow. Hans and Pete lead the regular team of ten dogs away from the traces.

(SOUND: snow squealing underfoot, odds shouted back and forth across the crowd)

Buck is put into the sled in his own harness. His coat shines with the sheen of silk; the mane half bristles with every movement. Men press in and feel the muscles rolling under the skin.

SKOOKUM BENCH KING
Gad, sir! Gad, sir! I offer you eight hundred for him, sir, before the test, sir; eight hundred just as he stands.

Thornton shakes his head and steps to Buck's side.

MATTHEWSON
You must stand off from him. Free play and plenty of room.

The crowd falls silent. Thornton kneels down by Buck, takes his head in his two hands and rests cheek on cheek. No shaking now, no love curses — only a whisper at the ear.

JOHN THORNTON
As you love me, Buck. As you love me.

Buck whines with suppressed eagerness. As Thornton gets to his feet, Buck seizes the mittened hand between his jaws, pressing in with his teeth and releasing slowly, half-reluctantly. Thornton steps well back.

(SOUND: nothing but wind and the creak of frozen leather)

JOHN THORNTON
Now, Buck.

Buck tightens the traces, then slacks them a matter of several inches.

JOHN THORNTON
Gee!

Buck swings to the right, ending the movement in a plunge that takes up the slack and arrests his one hundred and fifty pounds with a jerk. The load quivers.

(SOUND: a crisp crackling from under the runners)

JOHN THORNTON
Haw!

Buck duplicates the manoeuvre to the left. The sled pivots, runners slipping and grating sideways. Men hold their breath without knowing it.

(SOUND: the crackling turning to a hard snapping — the sled broken out)

JOHN THORNTON
Now, MUSH!

Buck throws himself forward. His whole body gathers compactly together, muscles writhing and knotting like live things under the silky fur, chest low to the ground, head forward and down, claws scarring the snow in parallel grooves. The sled sways, half-starts. One foot slips. A man groans aloud.

(SOUND: claws ripping packed snow; a single voice cracking out in the cold)

Half an inch. An inch. Two inches. The jerks diminish, the sled gains momentum, and then it is moving steadily along. Thornton runs behind, throwing short cheery words at him.

(SOUND: a cheer beginning small, growing, breaking into a roar as Buck passes the pile of firewood)

Hats and mittens fly in the air. Men shake hands with whoever is nearest, Matthewson among them.

Thornton falls on his knees beside Buck, head against head, shaking him back and forth, cursing him long and fervently and softly and lovingly. His eyes are wet; the tears stream frankly down his cheeks.

SKOOKUM BENCH KING
Gad, sir! Gad, sir! I'll give you a thousand for him, sir, a thousand, sir — twelve hundred, sir.

Thornton rises to his feet.

JOHN THORNTON
Sir, no, sir. You can go to hell, sir. It's the best I can do for you, sir.

Buck seizes Thornton's hand in his teeth. Thornton shakes him back and forth. As though animated by a common impulse, the onlookers draw back to a respectful distance.

(MUSIC: a warm swell rising over the crowd noise, then thinning to one held note)

EXT. STEWART RIVER TRAIL - DAY

A loaded sled cuts up a frozen river between upstanding peaks. Ammunition, pick, pan, a sack of salt. JOHN THORNTON walks behind the gee-pole. PETE and HANS flank the team. BUCK leads in the traces — one hundred and fifty pounds of him, stray brown on his muzzle and above his eyes, a splash of white running midmost down his chest.

(SOUND: sled runners hissing over river ice; harness leather creaking in the cold)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When Buck earned sixteen hundred dollars in five minutes, he made it possible for his master to pay off certain debts, and to journey with his partners into the East after a fabled lost mine.

The peaks close in. The river narrows to a streamlet under the snow.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Many men had sought it; few had found it; and more than a few there were who had never returned from the quest. But no living man had looted this treasure house, and the dead were dead.

EXT. ANCIENT BLAZED PATH IN THE FOREST - DAY

Summer. Axe scars, old and healed over with bark, mark a line of spruce trunks. Thornton runs a thumb across one. Buck sniffs the moss where the path simply stops.

(SOUND: wind combing the spruce tops; a woodpecker knocking far off)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The path began nowhere and ended nowhere, and it remained mystery, as the man who made it and the reason he made it remained mystery.

Deeper in: the time-graven wreckage of a hunting lodge. Hans lifts shreds of rotted blanket. Thornton draws out a long-barrelled flint-lock, cradles it, blows dust from the pan.

(SOUND: rotted timber giving under a boot)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The months came and went, and back and forth they twisted through the uncharted vastness, where no men were and yet where men had been, if the Lost Cabin were true.

EXT. PLACER CLAIM IN THE BROAD VALLEY - DAY

Spring. A shallow gravel bar in a broad valley. Thornton swirls a washing-pan and tips it — gold shows like yellow butter across the bottom. Pete whoops. Hans drops his shovel and grabs him.

(SOUND: gravel sluicing in water; a shovel biting into wet muck)

Moose-hide bags, fifty pounds to the bag, stand piled like so much firewood outside a spruce-bough lodge. The men toil like giants. The dogs lie idle in the sun.

(MUSIC: a hurried, glittering figure in strings, driving and repeating)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
They sought no farther. Each day they worked earned them thousands in clean dust and nuggets, and they worked every day.

EXT. SPRUCE-BOUGH LODGE CAMP - NIGHT

Firelight. Buck blinks at the coals, muzzle on his paws. Across the flame, half made of it — THE HAIRY MAN: short-legged, thick with hair, crouched with his head between his knees and his hands clasped above.

(SOUND: fire snapping; a branch flung onto the coals)

The hairy man starts awake, peers fearfully out into the darkness, throws on more wood, hugs his knees again.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The salient thing of this other world seemed fear.

The fire settles. The vision is only fire. Buck's ears cock up.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And closely akin to the visions of the hairy man was the call still sounding in the depths of the forest. It filled him with a great unrest and strange desires.

From the forest — a long-drawn howl, distinct and definite as never before.

(SOUND: a long-drawn howl, like, yet unlike, any noise made by husky dog)

Buck is on his feet, nostrils quivering, mane bristling in recurrent waves. He springs through the sleeping camp and is gone in swift silence.

EXT. FOREST CLEARING BY THE CREEK - NIGHT

An open place among the trees. Erect on haunches, nose pointed to the sky, sits WILD BROTHER — a long, lean timber wolf, gray, gaunt, in poor condition.

(SOUND: the howl breaking off mid-note; utter stillness)

Buck stalks into the open, half crouching, body gathered compactly together, tail straight and stiff, feet falling with unwonted care.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was the menacing truce that marks the meeting of wild beasts that prey.

The wolf bolts. Buck follows in wild leapings, runs him into a blind channel of the creek bed where a timber jam bars the way. The wolf whirls, pivoting on his hind legs, snarling.

(SOUND: teeth clipping together in continuous, rapid succession)

Buck does not attack. He circles, hedges him in with friendly advances. The wolf watches, darts, is cornered again. And again. At last he sniffs noses with Buck. They play about, half-coy, nervous.

Wild Brother starts off at an easy lope, looks back, makes it clear that Buck is to come.

EXT. LAND OF TIMBER AND STREAMS - DAY

Great stretches of forest and many streams. The two run side by side, hour after hour, unpacked earth underfoot, wide sky overhead. The sun climbs higher.

(SOUND: two sets of paws drumming soft ground; a stream gathering ahead)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He knew he was at last answering the call, running by the side of his wood brother toward the place from where the call surely came.

They stop at a running stream to drink. Water beads on Buck's muzzle. He sits down and does not drink again.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Old memories were coming upon him fast. And, stopping, Buck remembered John Thornton.

Wild Brother trots on, returns, sniffs noses, urges him. Buck turns about and starts slowly on the back track. For the better part of an hour the wolf runs at his side, whining softly. Then he sits, points his nose upward, and howls.

(SOUND: a mournful howl growing faint and fainter until it is lost in the distance)

EXT. SPRUCE-BOUGH LODGE CAMP - DAY

Thornton sits on a moose-hide sack, eating dinner. Buck bursts into camp and springs upon him, overturning him, scrambling on him, licking his face, biting his hand. The plate goes flying.

(SOUND: a tin plate clattering across gravel; a man's winded laughter)

Thornton shakes him back and forth, cursing him lovingly.

JOHN THORNTON
Playing the general tom-fool!

Two days pass. Buck follows Thornton at his work, watches him while he eats, sees him into his blankets at night and out of them in the morning.

On the third morning his head lifts, ears intent. He walks out of camp without looking back. The three men straighten and watch him go.

JOHN THORNTON
Never was there such a dog.

PETE
When he was made, the mould was broke.

HANS
Py jingo! I t'ink so mineself.

Inside the secrecy of the forest, Buck no longer marches. He steals along softly, cat-footed, a passing shadow among the shadows. He takes a ptarmigan from its nest without a sound.

(SOUND: brush closing behind him; the birdsong stopping)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He killed to eat, not from wantonness; but he preferred to eat what he killed himself.

EXT. DIVIDE AT THE HEAD OF THE CREEK - DAY

Fall. A band of twenty moose crowds the divide. Chief among them a great bull, standing over six feet from the ground, antlers branching to fourteen points. From his side, just forward of the flank, protrudes a feathered arrow-end.

(SOUND: the bull roaring with fury; hoofs cracking on frozen ground)

Buck barks and dances in front of him, just out of reach of the antlers and the splay hoofs. The bull charges; Buck retreats craftily, feigning an inability to escape. Two young bulls wheel back and the wounded bull rejoins the her

FADE OUT.

THE END
